# ARCHIVED 2026-10-20 (POS-729, Darko's go 14:53 EDT). Never installed, and superseded.
# The office runs its read workers as worker_threads inside the writer process:
# src/read-workers.mjs (POS-266), sized by OFFICE_READ_WORKERS (3 on prod). The box
# read of 2026-10-20 found no postmark-office-read@ unit, no postmark-reads.slice,
# no read-pool nginx conf and nothing listening on 4391-4395; the office's journal
# says "3 read workers". DEC-4's process-pool kit below was never applied.
# Kept whole for the story (CONVENTIONS rule 2). Do not install it.
#
# postmark-reads.slice — the read pool's CPU envelope (runbook DEC-4 / G3).
#
# PROPOSED. Not installed on the box by the lane that wrote it.
#
# ⚑ WHY A SLICE AND NOT A PER-UNIT QUOTA. The conductor's ask was "a CPUQuota
# (or the reason for none) so three workers + the writer + the timers cannot
# starve a crossing". A `CPUQuota=` on the TEMPLATE is a per-instance ceiling:
# three workers at 85% each bound the pool at 255% only when all three are busy,
# and throttle a lone worker to 85% when its two siblings are idle — which is
# most of the time, and which throttles exactly the reads the fourth core was
# bought to serve. A slice bounds the POOL as one thing: any single worker may
# use the whole envelope when its siblings are quiet, and the three together
# still cannot cross it. That is the shape the ask actually describes.
#
# ⚑ THE ARITHMETIC, on this box (4 cores = 400% total, measured `nproc`).
#
#     writer (postmark-office.service)     ~100%  under load; unbounded, and it
#                                                 must stay unbounded — it is
#                                                 the process that takes letters
#     read pool (this slice)                250%  ceiling
#     left for the twelve timers            ~50%  floor, and never zero
#
# Twelve postmark timers share these cores (`systemctl list-timers`, read
# 2026-09-08): rehydrate every 12 min, site-sentinel 5 min, usdc-watch 6 min,
# harbor-watch 6 min, stripe-watch 13 min, site-refresh 15 min,
# settlement-shadow 28 min, plus ferry, crossing-save (00:02 / 12:02 UTC),
# settlement (05:45 / 17:45 UTC), world2-notary and world2-backup. The two that
# must never be starved are crossing-save and settlement, and they are the two
# that run at fixed clock times rather than on an interval — a starved interval
# timer runs late, a starved crossing runs LATE AT A CROSSING, which residents
# see.
#
# ⚑ CPUWeight IS THE ONE THAT ACTUALLY PROTECTS THE CROSSING, and the quota is
# the backstop. A quota is a ceiling that binds whether or not anybody else
# wants the CPU; a weight only bites under contention, which is the only moment
# the crossing needs to win. At 50 against the default 100, the writer and every
# timer out-prioritise a read worker two to one exactly when they are competing,
# and the pool still gets the whole envelope on a quiet box.
#
# Reads today are served by ONE process pinned to ONE core. Even at this
# envelope the pool has 2.5 cores where it had 1, so the ceiling costs nothing
# anybody has today.
#
# Verified available on the box before proposing: systemd 255, cgroup2fs, and
# `cpu` present in /sys/fs/cgroup/cgroup.controllers — a CPUQuota on a slice is
# inert without the cpu controller, and a quota that silently does nothing is
# worse than no quota, because it reads as protection.

[Unit]
Description=Postmark office read workers (DEC-4 / G3) — bounded so a crossing cannot be starved
Before=slices.target

[Slice]
# The pool's ceiling, across all instances together.
CPUQuota=250%
# What decides who wins when the box is actually contended.
CPUWeight=50
# A read worker holds the office index and its memos; three of them plus the
# writer must not be able to push the box into swap and take the writer with
# them. 15.7 GB total on this box (`free -m`), the writer sits well under 1 GB.
MemoryMax=3G
